sections.body:
You've already seen the core Readable stream pattern, in the earlier core-modules concept's readBody helper: attach a 'data' listener (fired once per chunk, each one a Buffer by default) and an 'end' listener (fired once, after the last chunk, signaling there's nothing more to read):

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

const readable = fs.createReadStream('./large-file.txt');
let totalBytes = 0;

readable.on('data', (chunk) => {
  totalBytes += chunk.length;   // process this chunk without holding all of them
});

readable.on('end', () => {
  console.log(`Finished reading ${totalBytes} bytes.`);
});

readable.on('error', (err) => {
  console.error('Read failed:', err.message);
});
```

Attaching a 'data' listener switches the stream into **flowing mode**, where chunks arrive as fast as they're available, pushed to you via events. There's also a **paused mode**, where you explicitly call readable.read() to pull a chunk when you're ready for one -- useful for cases where you want to control the pace of consumption yourself rather than reacting to a firehose of events. Almost all everyday code either uses flowing mode directly (as above) or, more commonly still, never touches these events manually at all because .pipe() (covered next) manages the whole thing for you.

Notice the 'error' listener above is not optional in any serious sense: if a Readable stream emits an 'error' event with nothing listening for it, Node treats that the same as an uncaught exception -- covered in depth in the next module -- so a stream you forget to attach an error handler to can crash your whole process the moment something goes wrong (a missing file, a permissions error, a network drop).

A Writable stream exposes .write(chunk) to send data into it, and .end() to signal you're done writing (analogous to the res.end() you've already used on an HTTP response, which is itself a Writable stream):

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

const writable = fs.createWriteStream('./output.txt');
writable.write('First line\n');
writable.write('Second line\n');
writable.end('Final line\n');   // writes one more chunk, then closes

writable.on('finish', () => console.log('All data flushed to disk.'));
writable.on('error', (err) => console.error('Write failed:', err.message));
```

The detail that matters most here, and that sets up the entire next concept, is writable.write()'s **return value**: it returns true if the stream's internal buffer is comfortably under its configured threshold (its 'highWaterMark') and you're free to keep writing immediately, or **false** if the internal buffer is already full or over that threshold -- a signal that you should stop writing and wait for the stream to catch up before sending more. Ignoring that false return and continuing to call .write() anyway doesn't raise an error immediately, but it does mean the stream's internal buffer keeps growing without bound, which is precisely the mechanism behind the memory problems the next concept covers under the name 'backpressure.'

Manually wiring a Readable stream's 'data' events to a Writable stream's .write() calls, while correctly respecting write()'s return value and waiting for a 'drain' event when needed, is exactly the kind of repetitive, easy-to-get-subtly-wrong bookkeeping that .pipe() exists to automate:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

// Instead of manually reading chunks and writing them, just connect the two streams
fs.createReadStream('./large-file.txt')
  .pipe(fs.createWriteStream('./copy-of-large-file.txt'));
```

source.pipe(destination) does three things automatically: it forwards every chunk read from source into destination.write(), it pauses source whenever destination.write() returns false (respecting backpressure, without you writing that logic yourself), and it resumes source once destination emits 'drain'. This is the same pattern you'd use to stream a file directly into an HTTP response without ever loading the whole file into memory:

```javascript
const http = require('http');
const fs = require('fs');

http.createServer((req, res) => {
  fs.createReadStream('./large-video.mp4').pipe(res);
}).listen(3000);
```

Streams can also be chained through a Transform stream in the middle -- for instance, compressing a file on the fly while copying it, without ever holding the uncompressed *or* compressed version entirely in memory:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const zlib = require('zlib');

fs.createReadStream('./large-file.txt')
  .pipe(zlib.createGzip())
  .pipe(fs.createWriteStream('./large-file.txt.gz'));
```

There's a sharp edge to .pipe() worth knowing before it bites you in production: **it does not automatically forward error events between the streams it connects.** If the source stream in a .pipe() chain emits an error (say, the input file gets deleted mid-read), that error does not automatically propagate to the destination stream or anywhere else -- you still need an explicit .on('error', ...) listener on *each* stream in the chain, or that error becomes an unhandled 'error' event on whichever stream emitted it, which (as mentioned above) can crash the process. Chained pipes without individual error handlers on every link are a common, easy-to-miss source of production crashes that don't show up in casual local testing where nothing ever actually fails mid-stream.

Node's built-in stream.pipeline() function exists specifically to fix this ergonomics gap: it takes the same streams you'd normally chain with .pipe(), wires them together the same way, but funnels errors from *any* stream in the chain into a single callback (or, using its Promise-returning variant from stream/promises, into a single .catch()), and also correctly cleans up (destroying) every stream in the chain if any one of them fails -- something plain .pipe() does not reliably do on its own:

```javascript
const { pipeline } = require('stream/promises');
const fs = require('fs');
const zlib = require('zlib');

async function compressFile(input, output) {
  await pipeline(
    fs.createReadStream(input),
    zlib.createGzip(),
    fs.createWriteStream(output)
  );
  console.log('Compression finished successfully.');
}

compressFile('./large-file.txt', './large-file.txt.gz').catch((err) => {
  console.error('Pipeline failed:', err.message);
});
```

For any pipe chain with more than one stage, or any pipe chain running in production code rather than a quick throwaway script, pipeline() is the safer default over raw, manually chained .pipe() calls.

examples.code:
const fs = require('fs');

// Loads the ENTIRE file into memory before you can do anything with it
fs.readFile('./huge-log.txt', (err, data) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  console.log(`Loaded all ${data.length} bytes at once.`);
});

// Processes the file incrementally -- memory usage stays roughly constant
// regardless of the file's total size
let lineCount = 0;
let leftover = '';
fs.createReadStream('./huge-log.txt', { encoding: 'utf8' })
  .on('data', (chunk) => {
    const combined = leftover + chunk;
    const lines = combined.split('\n');
    leftover = lines.pop();   // last piece might be a partial line -- keep it for next chunk
    lineCount += lines.length;
  })
  .on('end', () => {
    if (leftover.length > 0) lineCount += 1;
    console.log(`Counted ${lineCount} lines without ever holding the whole file in memory.`);
  });

const http = require('http');
const fs = require('fs');

http.createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.url !== '/video') {
    res.statusCode = 404;
    return res.end('Not found');
  }

  const stream = fs.createReadStream('./demo.mp4');
  stream.on('error', (err) => {
    console.error('File stream error:', err.message);
    res.statusCode = 500;
    res.end('Server error');
  });
  stream.pipe(res);
}).listen(3000);

const { pipeline } = require('stream/promises');
const fs = require('fs');
const zlib = require('zlib');

async function decompressLogFile(gzPath, outPath) {
  try {
    await pipeline(
      fs.createReadStream(gzPath),
      zlib.createGunzip(),
      fs.createWriteStream(outPath)
    );
    console.log(`Decompressed ${gzPath} -> ${outPath}`);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Decompression pipeline failed:', err.message);
  }
}

decompressLogFile('./access.log.gz', './access.log');

